  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 AM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nTroughing will remain draped across the far NE CONUS and north \nAtlantic into this weekend as ridging folds over the northern Great \nPlains and Midwest, keeping the Great Lakes in northwest mid/upper-\nlevel flow between features over the next few days. Subsidence \nprovided at this inflection point will continue to build surface \nhigh pressure from Ontario down into the Great Lakes today and \nSaturday. Aforementioned ridging eventually slides east of the \nregion later this weekend as low-amplitude troughing slides \noverhead, supporting a cyclone that will trek across the Great Lakes \nlate Saturday through Sunday. High pressure may temporarily build \nback in early next week before a more active pattern settles in late \nin the forecast period.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nWith mostly sunny/clear skies in store for the Northwoods today into \nSaturday with high pressure in place, the primary focus turns to \nlate Saturday and Sunday when rain chances return to the region. \nGuidance indicates potential for split forcing from embedded \nshortwaves in the main flow -- one further south across the state \nborder/lower Great Lakes, and a second further north across Lake \nSuperior/Ontario. Should this be the case, northern Michigan may \nfind itself in a relative lull in rain coverage this weekend. \nEarliest chances look to arrive Saturday evening with best rain \nchances coming during the day on Sunday. Given this potential for \nsplit forcing, it is possible that some areas of northern Michigan \nend up seeing little/no rain into early next week. That said, the \nmost likely area to see steady rain during the second half of this \nweekend -- and low chances for localized heavy rainfall -- will be \nthe M-55 corridor and south. A few rumbles of thunder cannot be \nruled out Saturday night and Sunday.\n\nLooking ahead, dry weather looks to kick off next week before rain \nchances move back in closer to the middle of the week. High \ntemperatures in the upper 70s and low 80s early next week will be \nclose to seasonal averages before cooler daytime temperatures are \nexpected Wednesday.",
